--- a/release/src/router/www/TW.dict
+++ b/release/src/router/www/TW.dict
@@ -5404,7 +5404,7 @@ RQOS_04=Bandwidth
 RQOS_05=The shaper must own the bottleneck. Set upload to 90&ndash;95% of what a speed test <i>measures</i> (not the plan rate) &mdash; if this equals or exceeds what the line really delivers, the ISP buffer takes over and QoS barely engages.
 RQOS_06=Upload (obw)
 RQOS_07=Download (ibw)
-RQOS_08=Used by Traditional / Bandwidth Limiter / Cake. Hardware QoS shapes upload only.
+RQOS_08=Traditional / Bandwidth Limiter / Cake use both figures. Hardware QoS Classful polices download with the figure here; plain Hardware QoS (10) shapes upload only.
 RQOS_09=Classes &mdash; priority, guarantees, ceilings
 RQOS_10=Top row is served first (strict priority). <b>Min %</b> is a guaranteed floor under load; <b>Ceiling %</b> is the most a class may ever take. With the aggregate cap on, ceilings may total over 100% &mdash; they become burst allowances inside the capped upload.
 RQOS_100=Ceilings total
@@ -5584,6 +5584,13 @@ RQOS_112=No devices are capped yet. Add one below.
 RQOS_113=Pick a device or enter a MAC address (AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF).
 RQOS_114=Download and upload must each be at least 0.1 Mb/s.
 RQOS_115=You can limit up to 32 devices.
+RQOS_116=Download cap (ingress policer)
+RQOS_117=Polices WAN ingress at the download figure above, so heavy downloads back off before the ISP buffer fills &mdash; download bufferbloat control with hardware acceleration still on. A policer has no queue: a small share of packets is dropped early to signal senders, which is why the figure must be ~90% of what a speed test <i>measures</i>.
+RQOS_118=Wi-Fi downstream priority (WMM)
+RQOS_119=Stamps DiffServ onto LAN-bound traffic by class &mdash; Q1 to CS6 (voice airtime), Q2 to CS5 (video airtime), bulk Q5 to CS1 (background) &mdash; so Wi-Fi serves the same priorities on the download side. Wired clients are unaffected; a connection keeps the class it started with.
+RQOS_120=How does the download side work?
+RQOS_121=<p>Upload is shaped in hardware with per-class queues. Download has no hardware queue set on this silicon, so two mechanisms cover it: the <b>ingress policer</b> caps aggregate download before the ISP buffer can fill, and <b>WMM stamping</b> lets Wi-Fi transmit high classes first. Per-device download caps live in the Bandwidth Limiter; full download AQM needs Cake (which disables acceleration).</p>
+RQOS_122=No download figure is set, so the download cap will not engage. Apply anyway?
 RADV_72=Session ended - the advisor is now locked.
 NetworkTools_PktCap=Packet Capture
 NetworkTools_PktCap_desc=Capture live packets on an interface, bounded by packet count and time. A read-only diagnostic - no setting is changed.
@@ -5594,4 +5601,4 @@ NetworkTools_PktCap_filter=Capture filter (optional)
 NetworkTools_PktCap_btn=Start capture
 RADV_73=Allow network diagnostics
 RADV_74=Let the AI run bounded, read-only network probes (ping / traceroute / DNS / netstat) this session to help locate problems. It still cannot change any setting. Off by default.
-RTRF_71=Refresh: Live (200ms)
+RTRF_71=Refresh: Live (100ms)
